Global horizontal button-padding trim + undershoot contrast fix (2026-07-31)
===========================================================================

Two base.css changes, in chain order AFTER dashboard-row-geometry.patch
(which introduced the undershoot rule this patch then re-tunes).

--- 1. Horizontal button-padding trim ---------------------------------------

Standing user request, made several times ("напоминаю я уже нескольк ораз
просил их максимально сократить!"): cut padding as aggressively as possible
across the whole UI -- on a 320x240 panel padding is the biggest consumer of
the width labels need. Concrete bug it fixes: the More menu clipped
"Z Calibrate" to "Z Calibr...".

Which rule actually governed the clip (easy to get wrong): menu-grid buttons
carry menu.py's color1..color4 classes, and `button.color1..4
{ padding: 0.33em }` -- NOT the generic `button { padding: 0.25em }` -- is what
applied to them. 0.33em = 4.62px per side at the themes' 14px font.

  More grid: 4 columns over the ~282px content area = ~70.5px/cell. Per cell
  2 x .2em margin (5.6px) + 2 x .33em padding (9.2px) of chrome left ~55-63px
  of text width; the label wraps (WORD_CHAR, 2 lines) to "Z" / "Calibrate" and
  that second line needs ~66px, so it ellipsized. Three pixels short.

Change: horizontal padding .25em -> .1em on `button`, .33em -> .1em on
`button.color1..4`. Vertical padding untouched in both (it keeps a button's
icon off its own label, and vertical space is not the scarce dimension); the
coloured rule keeps its .1em bottom, tuned to sit above the border-bottom
accent. Returns 6.4px per coloured button / 4.2px per plain one BY CSS
ARITHMETIC -- that figure is derived, not measured; what was verified is the
rendering.

Why .1em and not 0: `button { margin: .2em }` already puts 5.6px between two
adjacent buttons; .1em per side widens the gap between their TEXT to 8.4px. At
0 the labels of side-by-side buttons would sit 5.6px apart and start to read
as one run of text -- button backgrounds here are the same colour as the
panel, so the gap and the coloured accent bar are the only separators.

Swept live after the change (all five surfaces the coordinator named):
  * More grid ....... "Z Calibrate" renders in full. FIXED. All 12 items
                      render un-truncated in the after frame.
  * Main dashboard .. unchanged (graph still x125 w193) -- the themes'
                      `.heater-grid button { padding: 0 .15em }` outspecifies
                      `button`, so this trim structurally cannot reach it.
  * Settings ........ unchanged (rows are labels + switches, not buttons).
  * Temperature ..... unchanged, INCLUDING its still-ellipsized "Heater B..."
                      / "OrangePi ...". Same `.heater-grid` specificity as the
                      dashboard -- a deliberate non-fix, not an unswept
                      surface. Reaching it would mean editing the themes'
                      heater-grid rule that panels/temperature.py depends on.
  * Move ............ unchanged; .1/.5/1/5/10/25/50 selector still fits.

Known cosmetic side effect, NOT fixable from base.css: "KlipperScreen" is a
single word with no break opportunity, so Pango char-wraps it wherever the
width runs out. The extra 6.4px moved the break from "Klipper-"/"Screen" to
"KlipperS-"/"creen" -- legible, not truncated, but uglier. Any width change
moves that break point. The real fix is one word in config/main_menu.conf
([menu __main more settings] name: KlipperScreen -> Settings).

--- 2. undershoot contrast follow-up ----------------------------------------

dashboard-row-geometry.patch restored undershoot as a 2px @active line. That
was WRONG in the light theme: @active is #8FB6D1 against @bg #EDEFF2, a pale
blue line on pale lavender, effectively invisible at 2px on a bottom row
already busy with content sliced mid-row.

This was not theoretical. agent-D reported gcode_macros.py "looks complete at
rest" AFTER the undershoot rule had already landed (agent-D's device window
06:44-06:59 UTC; the undershoot rule landed 06:12 UTC). Investigating showed
the line WAS drawing there all along -- it simply could not be seen. Since
this line is now the ONLY at-rest signal that a list continues (overlay
scrollbars fade, and GTK3 fades them via gtk_widget_set_opacity() +
gtk_widget_hide() on the scrollbar widget, which CSS cannot override),
"consistent with the scrollbar colour" is worth far less than "unmissable".

Change: alpha(@text, .45) instead of @active, and 2px -> 3px. @text is #1A1D21
in the light theme and white in the dark one, so the cue is a dark line on a
light background and a light line on a dark one -- guaranteed contrast in both
themes by construction rather than by an accent colour that happens to sit
close to the background in one of them.

Verified live at rest, scrolled to the top, mouse parked away from the list:
  * Macros (gcode_macros) .. line invisible before, clearly visible after.
                             Same frame, same scroll position -- the only
                             change is the colour/weight.
  * Settings ............... clearly visible at the bottom edge.
  * Console ................ plain Gtk.ScrolledWindow (not
                             CustomScrolledWindow); `undershoot` is a bare
                             element selector so it applies there too. Console
                             auto-scrolls, so it sits at the BOTTOM (no
                             undershoot.bottom by definition) and keeps its
                             scrollbar visible anyway -- it has a cue, but
                             undershoot.top was not clearly discernible in the
                             capture because that edge sits directly under the
                             button row. Flagging rather than claiming.

No !important anywhere; no GTK CSS warnings in the journal after restart.

diff --git a/styles/base.css b/styles/base.css
--- a/styles/base.css
+++ b/styles/base.css
@@ -118,11 +118,22 @@
     -gtk-icon-source: url("./styles/complete.svg");
 }
 
+/* Horizontal padding .25em -> .1em (2026-07-31, agent-B horizontal padding
+ * trim). Standing user request, made several times, to cut padding as hard as
+ * possible across the whole UI on this 320x240 panel. VERTICAL padding is
+ * deliberately left at .25em -- it is what keeps a button's icon off its own
+ * label, and vertical space is not the scarce dimension here.
+ * Why .1em (1.4px at the themes' 14px font) rather than 0: `margin: .2em`
+ * above already puts 5.6px of gap between two adjacent buttons; .1em of
+ * padding on each side widens the gap between their TEXT to 8.4px, which is
+ * what actually keeps "where does one button end" readable at this size.
+ * Going to 0 would leave the labels of side-by-side buttons 5.6px apart and
+ * start to read as one run of text. */
 button {
     background-image: none;
     background-color: @bg;
     margin: .2em;
-    padding: 0.25em;
+    padding: 0.25em 0.1em;
     border-radius: 0;
     border: 0;
     -gtk-icon-shadow: none;
@@ -152,12 +163,24 @@
     color: grey;
 }
 
+/* Same trim as `button` above, and THE fix for the clipped "Z Calibr..." in
+ * the More menu (2026-07-31, agent-B horizontal padding trim). These four
+ * classes are what menu.py puts on every menu-grid button, and this rule --
+ * not the generic `button` rule -- is the one that governs them: .33em is
+ * 4.62px per side at 14px. The More grid is 4 columns over the ~282px content
+ * area = ~70.5px per cell; after 2x .2em margin and 2x .33em padding only
+ * ~55-63px of text width was left, and the wrapped second line "Calibrate"
+ * needs ~66px, so it ellipsized to "Calibr...". Dropping the horizontal
+ * padding to .1em returns 6.4px per button -- that figure is CSS ARITHMETIC
+ * (.33em - .1em = 3.22px per side at 14px), not a measurement; what was
+ * actually verified is the rendering, which now shows "Z Calibrate" in full.
+ * Vertical padding (.33em top / .1em bottom, the latter tuned to sit above
+ * the coloured border-bottom accent) is untouched. */
 button.color1,
 button.color2,
 button.color3,
 button.color4 {
-    padding: 0.33em;
-    padding-bottom: 0.1em;
+    padding: 0.33em 0.1em 0.1em 0.1em;
 }
 
 button.color1 {
@@ -361,16 +384,30 @@
  * mechanism Adwaita itself uses for its dashed default, so it is guaranteed
  * supported by GTK3's CSS engine) rather than Adwaita's dashes, which are
  * too faint to read on a 320x240 panel. */
+/* Colour/weight contrast follow-up (2026-07-31): the first version of this
+ * rule drew the line in @active. That is the scrollbar slider's colour, which
+ * looked consistent -- but in the LIGHT theme @active is #8FB6D1 against @bg
+ * #EDEFF2, i.e. a pale blue line on a pale lavender background, which at 2px
+ * is genuinely hard to see on a panel whose bottom row is already busy with
+ * content sliced mid-row. Since this line is now the ONLY at-rest signal that
+ * a list continues (overlay scrollbars fade out, and GTK3 does that by
+ * hiding the scrollbar widget, which CSS cannot override), "consistent with
+ * the scrollbar" is worth less than "unmissable".
+ * alpha(@text, .45) instead: @text is #1A1D21 in the light theme and white in
+ * the dark one, so the cue is a dark line on a light background and a light
+ * line on a dark one -- guaranteed contrast in BOTH themes by construction,
+ * rather than depending on an accent colour that happens to be close to the
+ * background in one of them. Raised 2px -> 3px for the same reason. */
 undershoot.top {
-    background-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, @active, @active);
-    background-size: 100% 2px;
+    background-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, alpha(@text, .45), alpha(@text, .45));
+    background-size: 100% 3px;
     background-repeat: no-repeat;
     background-position: center top;
 }
 
 undershoot.bottom {
-    background-image: linear-gradient(to top, @active, @active);
-    background-size: 100% 2px;
+    background-image: linear-gradient(to top, alpha(@text, .45), alpha(@text, .45));
+    background-size: 100% 3px;
     background-repeat: no-repeat;
     background-position: center bottom;
 }
